{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Let nothing be hidden from my lord, etc.</b> It is difficult: what is their intention in saying <b>let nothing be hidden</b>? And how is it possible to hide a matter that stands to be clarified — for if they were to say that it is not exhausted, they would need to bring it? And what is this trust that they declare to him, that they will not hide the matter? <b>Further</b>, one needs to understand their saying <b>ki im</b> ('since'), which has no [clear] meaning. <b>Further</b>, did they not already tell him in the first year that the silver was gone? <b>Further</b>, one needs to understand the intent of their saying <b>and the cattle, etc., to my lord</b>." "Perhaps they intended to say in this manner: <b>let nothing be hidden</b>, etc. — meaning, we have no silver and we are hiding it from my lord, for the truth is that the silver is exhausted. And their intention is that he not suspect them of feigning poverty and coming to request by way of charity. And their saying <b>and the cattle to my lord</b> — meaning, whatever there is of cattle belongs to my lord, for he set no boundary on the cattle but rather acquired everything in a general way, as he said: ״וַיְנַהֲלֵם בְּכָל מִקְנֵיהֶם״ ('and he acquired them with all their cattle'). Therefore they said that they needed to say ״let nothing be hidden״ only regarding silver, which is a thing that can be concealed; but as for cattle — whatever they have belongs to my lord; wherever they are, they are his. And their saying <b>there is nothing left</b>, etc., is to say that they have neither silver nor anything worth silver." "<b>And according to</b> what I explained in the verse ״כִּי אָפֵס כָּסֶף״ in a second manner — that they claimed they had [silver] but it was not available in their hands — the verse is resolved properly: for they came and said to him that they would hide nothing from him, unlike the first time when they made a false claim that they had [silver] but it was withheld from them — not so, for <b>ki im</b> ('since') it is truly ״תַּם״ (exhausted) — meaning, it is finished and they have no more silver. And through this it is deducible why the first time they said ״אָפֵס״ ('gone') and the second time they said ״תַּם״ ('exhausted')." "And their saying <b>and the cattle to my lord</b> — meaning, the cattle that until now they had relied upon until silver would become available, from now on it shall be fully transferred to you; and this is what he said: ״to my lord.״"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:49:42.405812590Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}